!! Structural editing (write-back to real tiddlers)

When the active producer declares `capabilities.structural: true` (e.g. `knowledge-tree`), drag-reparent / rename / +child / Delete in the mindmap mutate the underlying tiddlers directly. The two settings below tune that behaviour.

!!! Default type for new nodes

When the user creates a node via Tab/Enter, the new tiddler's `kn.type` field is chosen by this strategy:

* `fixed:<type>` — always use the literal type (e.g. `fixed:note`, `fixed:idea`).
* `derive-from-parent` — read the parent node's `kn.type`. Falls back to the default below when the parent has none.

|!Tiddler |`$:/config/rimir/mindmap/structural/new-node-type-strategy` |
|!Default |`fixed:note` |

''Current value:''
<$edit-text tiddler="$:/config/rimir/mindmap/structural/new-node-type-strategy" tag="input" default="fixed:note" placeholder="fixed:note"/>

Quick presets:

* <$button class="rr-link-btn"><$action-setfield $tiddler="$:/config/rimir/mindmap/structural/new-node-type-strategy" text="fixed:note"/>fixed:note</$button>
* <$button class="rr-link-btn"><$action-setfield $tiddler="$:/config/rimir/mindmap/structural/new-node-type-strategy" text="fixed:idea"/>fixed:idea</$button>
* <$button class="rr-link-btn"><$action-setfield $tiddler="$:/config/rimir/mindmap/structural/new-node-type-strategy" text="derive-from-parent"/>derive-from-parent</$button>

!!! Cascade-confirm threshold

Reparent / delete / rename operations whose effect spans more than this many descendant tiddlers pop a confirm modal before applying. Set to a very high number to disable the confirmation entirely.

|!Tiddler |`$:/config/rimir/mindmap/structural/cascade-threshold` |
|!Default |`10` |

''Current value:''
<$edit-text tiddler="$:/config/rimir/mindmap/structural/cascade-threshold" tag="input" type="number" default="10"/>

!!! Visible-label field

The bottom-right actions panel includes a small dropdown that picks the tiddler field used as the visible node label. Picking `title` keeps the current behaviour: node names follow the leaf-segment of the title (drag-rename renames the tiddler, references cascade via `flibbles/relink-titles`). Picking any other field — typically `caption` — decouples //display// from //structural identity//:

* drag-rename writes the typed value to the chosen field; the title is preserved (no cascade).
* `+child` / `+sibling` still derives a sanitized title slug from the typed value, AND stamps the typed value on the chosen field of the new tiddler.
* reparent still cascades structurally (titles change, references update) — only the label source is decoupled.
* empty field on the source tiddler falls back to the leaf segment (`caption` mode, marked `mm:label-derived`) or to the literal `UNDEFINED` (any other field, marked `mm:label-status`).

The list of pickable fields is taken from this config tiddler, one field per line:

|!Tiddler |`$:/config/rimir/mindmap/label-fields` |
|!Default |`title`<br/>`caption` |

''Current value:''
<$edit-text tiddler="$:/config/rimir/mindmap/label-fields" tag="textarea" default={{{ [[title]] +[addsuffix[\n]addsuffix[caption]] }}} placeholder="title&#10;caption&#10;kn.caption"/>

The choice is stored in `$:/state/rimir/mindmap/<key>/label-field` (session-scoped — lost on reload). A view tiddler can set `mm.label-field` for a stable per-view default.

!! Slides-only view (canvas toolbar)

When the active producer supports it (`knowledge-tree` does; `capabilities.slides: true`), the canvas toolbar carries a ''Slides only'' toggle. Pressed = the tree is pruned to nodes that have at least one slide, plus any ancestor needed to keep them connected to the root. Released = the full tree returns.

The choice lives in `$:/state/rimir/mindmap/<key>/slides-only` — `$:/state/`, so it doesn't sync and doesn't persist across reloads. Slides-only is a transient view mode; if you want a durable "this view always shows slides only" preference, file an issue and we'll add it.

Composes with focus mode and label-field — focus re-roots first, then slides-only prunes the focused subtree, then label-field rewrites the visible labels.

!! Slide authoring (right pane)

The preview pane carries a modebar above its content with the selected tiddler's title on the left and a unified dropdown on the right — ''Body'' / ''Slides'' / ''<each presentation>'' / ''+ New presentation…''. Switching to ''Slides'' replaces the body preview with a list of slide cards for the selected node — add, edit (inline), move up/down, delete.

Each slide is its own tiddler at `<owner>/slides/<slug>`:

|!Field |!Value |
|`kn.type` |`slide` |
|tags |`$:/tags/rimir/mindmap/slide` |
|`mm.slide-of` |Owner tiddler title (back-reference; relink-titles cascades on owner-rename) |
|`mm.slide-layout` |`default` / `title` / `bullets` / any custom CSS class |
|`mm.slide-notes` |Single-line presenter notes |
|caption |Optional display name shown in the slide list |
|text |Slide body — plain wikitext |

The owner tiddler's `mm.slide-order` TW list field carries the slides in display / playback order. When the owner is renamed via the structural editing flow, `flibbles/relink-titles` automatically renames the whole `<owner>/slides/<*>` subtree, so the slide references in `mm.slide-order` survive the rename without manual fix-up.

''Recognised layouts:''

* `default` — full-bleed wikitext content (no extra style).
* `title` — centred large heading (CSS class `rr-mindmap-slide-content-title`).
* `bullets` — tighter bullet list styling (CSS class `rr-mindmap-slide-content-bullets`).
* Any custom value — used verbatim as a CSS class (`rr-mindmap-slide-content-<value>`); style it yourself.

''+ Add slide:''

Clicking ''+ Add slide'' creates a blank slide tiddler under the selected node (the `rr-mindmap-add-slide` widget message handles the creation + slug uniquification) and opens it inline in edit mode — type the caption / pick a layout / write the body, and every keystroke writes through (no Save button; ''Done'' just collapses the editor back to view mode).

''Slide exclusion:''

The `knowledge-tree` producer skips slide-tagged tiddlers (both in the produced MDOM and the watched-titles filter). Slides are content owned by their parent node, not structural nodes in the tree.

''Session state:''

* `$:/state/rimir/mindmap/<key>/inspect-mode` — Body / Slides / Presentation
* `$:/state/rimir/mindmap/<key>/slide-editing` — title of the slide currently in edit mode (empty = list view)

Both are `$:/state/` titles, so they don't sync.

!! Named presentations (Phase 2)

A ''presentation'' is a playlist: an ordered list of tiddler titles whose slides will be walked in sequence during playback. Presentations live as their own tiddlers, decoupled from any one view-level overlay — multiple presentations can target the same view, and the same node can appear in multiple presentations.

A presentation tiddler:

* is tagged `$:/tags/rimir/mindmap/presentation`
* has field `mm.view` — the view tiddler this presentation belongs to
* has field `mm.slides-order` — TW list of tiddler titles
* may have fields `mm.transition` and `mm.duration` for the (still planned) player

The currently-active presentation for a view is referenced by the view tiddler's `mm.presentation` field — a regular tiddler field, so the choice survives reloads.

''Authoring flow:''

# The preview-pane modebar dropdown (above the right pane) unifies mode and presentation choice. Options: ''Body'', ''Slides'', and under a ''Presentations'' optgroup each existing presentation for this view plus ''+ New presentation…''.
# Picking ''+ New presentation…'' prompts for a name, sanitises a slug under `presentations/<slug>`, creates the tiddler with the right tags and fields, sets it active on the view, and switches inspect-mode to "presentation".
# Picking an existing presentation atomically activates it (`mm.presentation`) AND switches to "presentation" mode. Picking Body/Slides switches mode without disturbing the active presentation.
# With a presentation active, selecting a node in the canvas reveals a ''+ Add / − Remove'' button in the bottom-right actions panel — toggles the selected tiddler's title in `mm.slides-order`.
# In Presentation mode the right pane shows the playlist: each member node's title (clickable — focuses it on the canvas) plus its slide tiddlers (via `[<nodeTitle>mm-slides[]]`), rendered live with their layout class.

''Lifecycle:''

* Presentations coexist — picking `[none]` deactivates without deleting. Switching presentations is non-destructive.
* Deleting a presentation tiddler removes it from the toolbar dropdown automatically (the widget watches presentation-tagged tiddlers).
* Renaming a tiddler in `mm.slides-order` does NOT cascade — Phase 3 will register the field with flibbles/relink for rename cascade.

Phase 3 will add the ''presentation creation mode'' (visual fading of non-included nodes on the canvas, drag-to-reorder, per-slide transition overrides) and the ''fullscreen player'' (sequential walk with keyboard navigation).

!! Overlay tiddler titles and persistence

Drag/rename/collapse changes are stored in the overlay tiddler named by the view's `mm.overlay` field (or the `overlay=` widget attribute). For changes to survive browser reloads and server restarts, that title MUST be //synced to the server// — that is, it MUST match the wiki's `$:/config/SyncFilter`.

The TiddlyWiki core default excludes anything under `$:/state/`, `$:/temp/`, `$:/status/` and `$:/HistoryList`. So:

* `Mindmap/<view>/overlay` — //persists// (regular tiddler, written to disk)
* `$:/config/rimir/mindmap/overlays/<view>` — //persists// (system but synced)
* `$:/state/...` — ''lost on reload''. Only use this for ephemeral session state.

If your wiki has a custom `$:/config/SyncFilter`, mirror that here when naming overlays.

!! Overlay write debounce

Overlay tiddler writes are debounced at 250 ms to avoid spamming filesystem-watcher and git-int with every intermediate drag event. This is currently hard-coded; if you need to tune it, file an issue.

!! Restricted-wiki environments

If `llm-connect` or another plugin restricts which tiddlers may be written, overlay tiddler titles MUST be in the protection allow filter or writes will be silently dropped. The widget reflects this with no error — drag changes appear to work in the UI but do not persist after reload.
